00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:52.462
SPEAKER 1: In this demo,
we have a knowledge base

00:03:52.462 --> 00:03:57.540
that contains Wikipedia articles
about a wide variety of topics.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:59.402
In all, there are 48 documents.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:05.210
One of the documents
is about steam engines.

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:07.415
So here you see the content
of the Wikipedia article

00:04:07.415 --> 00:04:08.290
about a steam engine.

00:04:11.875 --> 00:04:14.250
I would say I want to know
something about steam engines.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:22.810
We see in our basic search
we have a result that

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:25.240
matches on some key words,
but it's not semantically

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:27.700
similar to the query.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.980
We go to the semantic match.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:34.280
We see that the answer matches
the meaning of the query.

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:46.800
If we view it in the document,
we can see where in the article

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:48.540
the passage is coming from.

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:55.190
Another one of our
articles is about Doctor

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:57.050
Who, a famous British TV show.

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:04.550
Let's say I want to know
