to show you how that all works.

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:40.720
So the platform, there
are really, sort of,

00:15:40.720 --> 00:15:42.220
three steps, if you will.

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:44.020
First is ingestion.

00:15:44.020 --> 00:15:46.720
For us ingestion
is really broad.

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:49.840
So we have customers who store
physical information with us.

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:52.180
Could be paper, could be tapes.

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:53.950
And we want to make
sure that we can

00:15:53.950 --> 00:15:57.730
allow them to pull all of that
information into the platform

00:15:57.730 --> 00:16:00.340
in order to enrich
it and process it.

00:16:00.340 --> 00:16:02.680
It could also be digital
information, right?

00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:06.670
So ingestion from various
different sources,

00:16:06.670 --> 00:16:11.560
from various different types or
forms, and in various formats.

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:16.210
Video, images, documents,
all of those various sources

00:16:16.210 --> 00:16:19.190
that Sudherra mentioned earlier.

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:23.590
Second step really then is all
about processing that content.

00:16:23.590 --> 00:16:27.130
We built a cloud native
platform on top of GCP.

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:30.850
So we're leveraging
all the power of GCP

00:16:30.850 --> 00:16:33.820
in order to store and
process the information.

00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:36.760
We're using Document
Understanding AI in order

00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:40.240
to classify and
enrich the content.

00:16:40.240 --> 00:16:44.800
And really it's all about three
primary use cases, if you will,

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:47.260
at a high level that
we want to drive.

00:16:47.260 --> 00:16:49.060
One is around analytics.

00:16:49.060 --> 00:16:53.600
How do I now make this
information valuable to a user?

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:56.030
The second is around
information governance.

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:58.480
Now that I know what I
have, what are the policies

00:16:58.480 --> 00:16:59.470
that I need to apply?

00:16:59.470 --> 00:17:01.270
How long should I keep it?

00:17:01.270 --> 00:17:03.070
When do I need to
protect it because it

00:17:03.070 --> 00:17:06.349
has personal or sensitive
information in it?

00:17:06.349 --> 00:17:09.130
And then the third one is
around workflow automation.

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:11.079
And we'll walk
through use cases that

00:17:11.079 --> 00:17:13.374
illustrate each of those items.

00:17:16.750 --> 00:17:18.970
In the end, it's
about adding structure

00:17:18.970 --> 00:17:21.910
to that big portion
of your content

00:17:21.910 --> 00:17:26.089
that is today unstructured.

00:17:26.089 --> 00:17:28.990
So the first use case
is in oil and gas.

00:17:28.990 --> 00:17:33.160
So we store incredibly
varying types

00:17:33.160 --> 00:17:34.900
of things for our oil
and gas customers.

00:17:34.900 --> 00:17:38.920
Could be ginormous maps that
get scanned and then processed.

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:41.500
Could be seismic
data that is on tape.

00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:44.680
Could be well logs that could
be dozens to hundreds of feet

00:17:44.680 --> 00:17:45.730
long.

00:17:45.730 --> 00:17:48.820
And we help A, ingest
all of that information.

00:17:48.820 --> 00:17:51.790
And then using the
Document Understanding AI,

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:53.950
we extract
information that helps

00:17:53.950 --> 00:17:56.560
us, frankly, enable
the geoscientist

00:17:56.560 --> 00:17:58.520
to make use of this information.

00:17:58.520 --> 00:18:00.550
So all of the various
content, can you

00:18:00.550 --> 00:18:02.080
imagine if you're
a scientist trying

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:04.750
to find the information
you need to evaluate where

00:18:04.750 --> 00:18:08.500
you might want to drill or what
the value of a certain well is?

00:18:08.500 --> 00:18:10.750
You're going across paper
maps at Iron Mountain.

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:13.950
You're going to tape
potentially for seismic.

00:18:13.950 --> 00:18:16.120
And one of the challenges
is you just don't ever

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:18.130
know whether you
actually got access

00:18:18.130 --> 00:18:19.760
to everything that you need.

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:22.480
So what we're doing here is
processing the information,

00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:25.390
again using Document
Understanding AI.

00:18:25.390 --> 00:18:27.400
We also can enrich
the information

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:29.240
with external metadata sources.

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:32.890
So it's about pulling as
much information metadata

00:18:32.890 --> 00:18:37.990
together from using AI, as
well as from existing sources,

00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:41.800
and putting that all
together in a visual search

00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:45.970
UI that allows geoscientists
to search for it in a way that

00:18:45.970 --> 00:18:47.020
makes sense.

00:18:47.020 --> 00:18:50.110
So instead of spending half
your time looking for the stuff,

00:18:50.110 --> 00:18:53.830
you can now spend most of your
time actually analyzing it.

00:18:53.830 --> 00:18:56.750
So the search UI allows
access by location.

00:18:56.750 --> 00:18:59.260
So if I'm looking for a
certain spot on the map

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:00.970
that I want to
evaluate, I can get

00:19:00.970 --> 00:19:04.440
right to the information
that's pertinent to me

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:07.540
across the various assets
that I'm dealing with.

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:09.910
We use, for example,
a similarity search.

00:19:09.910 --> 00:19:13.420
So that if I see a certain
pattern or a sort of intrusion

00:19:13.420 --> 00:19:14.860
that might be of
interest to me, I

00:19:14.860 --> 00:19:18.980
can quickly find all the assets
that have a similar pattern.

00:19:18.980 --> 00:19:23.500
And all of that really is
about faster decision making

00:19:23.500 --> 00:19:25.840
and better analysis.

00:19:29.610 --> 00:19:34.070
Second use case, a bit
more text document heavy.

00:19:34.070 --> 00:19:36.860
In this case, we're
dealing with mortgage

00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:41.000
and really enabling that
automation of a very, very

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:42.580
manual workflow.

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:45.350
We all might think that this
stuff is all online now.

00:19:45.350 --> 00:19:46.550
We fill it all out online.

00:19:46.550 --> 00:19:47.870
You'd be surprised.

00:19:47.870 --> 00:19:51.750
Most of it becomes paper
somewhere along the line.

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:55.730
So it's a very, very
heavy, manual process.

00:19:55.730 --> 00:19:59.430
And as a result, it's
also a very slow process.

00:19:59.430 --> 00:20:01.610
So this particular
use case is all

00:20:01.610 --> 00:20:07.910
about taking a process from
multiple days to hours.

00:20:07.910 --> 00:20:10.520
For this particular
use case, it's

00:20:10.520 --> 00:20:13.800
also very illustrative of the
power of Document Understanding

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:14.780
AI.

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:17.900
What we're doing here is
we're looking at a loan file

00:20:17.900 --> 00:20:20.010
and we're asking two questions.

00:20:20.010 --> 00:20:22.520
Is it complete and
is it accurate?

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:25.850
So in order to decide whether
a mortgage balance is complete,

00:20:25.850 --> 00:20:28.950
I got to understand
what documents I got.

00:20:28.950 --> 00:20:30.380
Classify the documents.

00:20:30.380 --> 00:20:33.530
Compare against a
list of per location

00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:35.120
what's actually required.

00:20:35.120 --> 00:20:37.260
What defines complete.

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:39.800
Making sure that all
those documents are here.

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:41.430
Now we dig in.

00:20:41.430 --> 00:20:43.020
Is it accurate?

00:20:43.020 --> 00:20:45.230
So this is where we have
to pull information.

00:20:45.230 --> 00:20:48.330
Whether it's a name, a
social security number.

00:20:48.330 --> 00:20:49.453
It could be an APR.

00:20:49.453 --> 00:20:50.870
There are lots,
and lots, and lots

00:20:50.870 --> 00:20:53.150
of extractions
that happen across

00:20:53.150 --> 00:20:54.890
those different documents.

00:20:54.890 --> 00:20:56.090
And two things.

00:20:56.090 --> 00:20:58.700
One, do I have
everything filled out?

00:20:58.700 --> 00:21:01.070
Do I have a signature where
I'm expecting a signature?

00:21:01.070 --> 00:21:03.950
Do I have a name where
I'm expecting a name?

00:21:03.950 --> 00:21:09.320
Do I have a stamp from a certain
maybe regulatory group that

00:21:09.320 --> 00:21:11.270
needs to bless something.

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:13.460
All of those things
need to get checked.

00:21:13.460 --> 00:21:15.560
Then I need to look
across the documents.

00:21:15.560 --> 00:21:18.170
Do I have the same name with
the same social security

00:21:18.170 --> 00:21:19.190
number in all of them?

00:21:19.190 --> 00:21:20.930
Is the APR the same?

00:21:20.930 --> 00:21:24.710
And essentially identifying,
did somebody put the wrong paper

00:21:24.710 --> 00:21:28.430
in the wrong file or is a
document filled in incorrectly

00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:30.110
or incompletely?

00:21:30.110 --> 00:21:34.620
So document understanding helps
us to do all of those things.

00:21:34.620 --> 00:21:37.350
And then from a workflow
automation perspective,

00:21:37.350 --> 00:21:41.060
we can output that to
enable an RPA process that

00:21:41.060 --> 00:21:47.850
now can be much, much more
automated, rather than manual.

00:21:47.850 --> 00:21:50.630
So when you're
thinking about the top

00:21:50.630 --> 00:21:54.980
of this particular slide,
all those little people,

00:21:54.980 --> 00:21:56.940
that's where all the
time is being spent.

00:21:56.940 --> 00:21:59.480
That's where the inefficiency
comes in for the processor.

00:21:59.480 --> 00:22:02.300
That's where the wait time comes
in from a customer experience

00:22:02.300 --> 00:22:03.120
perspective.

00:22:03.120 --> 00:22:03.620
All right?

00:22:03.620 --> 00:22:07.220
So we're really looking for
here is to drive better customer

00:22:07.220 --> 00:22:11.360
experience for the
actual applicant,

00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:15.320
as well as time and cost
savings for the processor.

00:22:15.320 --> 00:22:19.150
And then finally, of
course, the automation

00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:25.280
to enable the entire workflow
to be automated end to end.

00:22:25.280 --> 00:22:27.710
The second thing that
is really important

00:22:27.710 --> 00:22:30.780
is once we have all of
this rich information,

00:22:30.780 --> 00:22:34.130
there are other use cases
that can be enabled.

00:22:34.130 --> 00:22:37.130
So if a mortgage processor
wants to understand

00:22:37.130 --> 00:22:42.290
how they are doing with respect
to compliance for fair lending,

00:22:42.290 --> 00:22:44.090
they have all this
rich information

00:22:44.090 --> 00:22:47.150
that they can now use to
understand how they're doing.

00:22:47.150 --> 00:22:49.460
Are they actually
well positioned

00:22:49.460 --> 00:22:50.780
or do they have an issue?

00:22:50.780 --> 00:22:52.270
And what can they do about it?

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:56.090
So the analytics on top, once
we've done all of this work,

00:22:56.090 --> 00:22:58.160
there are a myriad
of other use cases

00:22:58.160 --> 00:23:00.350
that can be enabled for
that particular customer.

00:23:03.220 --> 00:23:07.560
Another very document-centric
process is HR.

00:23:07.560 --> 00:23:09.570
So employee records.

00:23:09.570 --> 00:23:13.020
This particular use case
is focused on privacy.

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:15.540
So GDPR was mentioned.

00:23:15.540 --> 00:23:19.320
So personal information
and complying with GDPR

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:22.650
really means you have to
be able to understand where

00:23:22.650 --> 00:23:25.170
you have personal information
about your employees,

00:23:25.170 --> 00:23:26.760
in this case.

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:30.270
And you have to be able
to get to it quickly.

00:23:30.270 --> 00:23:32.100
An employee says,
hey, I want to know

00:23:32.100 --> 00:23:34.650
what you are keeping about me.

00:23:34.650 --> 00:23:38.160
What personal information
you're storing of mine.

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:41.040
I need to be able to get
to that content quickly.

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:44.282
I need to be sure that
I'm finding all of it.

00:23:44.282 --> 00:23:45.990
And I need to be able
to actually give it

00:23:45.990 --> 00:23:48.120
to the employee,
if they ask for it.

00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:53.640
So processing documents
from various sources

00:23:53.640 --> 00:23:55.050
and in various formats.

00:23:55.050 --> 00:23:58.290
So I've been at Iron Mountain
for a really long time.

00:23:58.290 --> 00:24:01.860
When I first got hired,
we filled out paper.

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:06.210
So my records
probably are in paper.

00:24:06.210 --> 00:24:10.440
So pulling all of that in, being
able to image that content,

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:15.030
and allow that to be commingled
with content that may have been

00:24:15.030 --> 00:24:18.960
collected electronically just
a couple of months ago or last

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:21.120
year is very, very important.

00:24:21.120 --> 00:24:24.480
And being able to
have the ability

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:28.410
to understand where that
content in the document

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:30.690
is and whether or not
I'm actually dealing

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:33.150
with sensitive information
or personal information,

00:24:33.150 --> 00:24:36.240
as opposed to just, here's a big
pile of stuff that may or may

00:24:36.240 --> 00:24:39.330
not be relevant, again,
enables companies

00:24:39.330 --> 00:24:43.260
to not only be compliant, but
be efficient in responding

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:45.840
to requests.

00:24:45.840 --> 00:24:47.730
So the other thing
about these things

00:24:47.730 --> 00:24:51.720
is you can't keep
stuff forever anymore.

00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:53.280
So retention policy.

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:57.870
A lot of companies for
a long time the default

00:24:57.870 --> 00:24:59.400
was, well, I keep
everything forever

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:02.190
so that way I'm not deleting
anything that I shouldn't be.

00:25:02.190 --> 00:25:09.300
Well, GDPR actually ends the era
of everything forever retention

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:10.360
policy.

00:25:10.360 --> 00:25:12.240
So now that we have
this information,

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:14.040
we have it classified
we understand

00:25:14.040 --> 00:25:17.100
who it's for, when an
employee leaves the company,

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:18.570
we have an obligation
to actually

00:25:18.570 --> 00:25:20.610
be able to get rid
of that information

00:25:20.610 --> 00:25:22.580
within the retention period.

00:25:22.580 --> 00:25:27.900
That is, within the compliance
for the privacy regulation.

00:25:27.900 --> 00:25:30.300
So classifying the
content, understanding

00:25:30.300 --> 00:25:32.850
that it is employee
information that

00:25:32.850 --> 00:25:34.650
pertains to a
certain employee now

00:25:34.650 --> 00:25:39.100
also allows us to get rid of
the information when we need to.

00:25:39.100 --> 00:25:43.560
So that's where the policy
plus Document Understanding AI

00:25:43.560 --> 00:25:47.760
really comes together to help
companies do the right thing

00:25:47.760 --> 00:25:48.690
at the right time.

00:25:51.310 --> 00:25:55.010
This next one I'm going to
dig into a little bit deeper.

00:25:55.010 --> 00:25:58.290
Actually we hear it a
lot from our customers.

00:25:58.290 --> 00:26:01.080
it Doesn't really matter
what kind of company you are.

00:26:01.080 --> 00:26:02.880
You probably have
customer contracts.

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:05.110
We all hope we have many, right?

00:26:05.110 --> 00:26:08.670
At Iron Mountain, that's
something like 400,000.

00:26:08.670 --> 00:26:12.450
So very simple things
get really hard when

00:26:12.450 --> 00:26:16.110
you're trying to do them across
a big, huge pile of stuff

00:26:16.110 --> 00:26:17.550
like that.

00:26:17.550 --> 00:26:21.450
So first of all, they tend
to be kind of distributed.

00:26:21.450 --> 00:26:24.250
Different products and
services, different regions,

00:26:24.250 --> 00:26:27.180
different acquisitions that
they may have come from.

00:26:27.180 --> 00:26:28.890
So the stuff is
all over the place.

00:26:28.890 --> 00:26:31.350
So simple, simple
questions like,

00:26:31.350 --> 00:26:34.380
what are all the contracts
for this particular customer?

00:26:34.380 --> 00:26:35.790
Already hard.

00:26:35.790 --> 00:26:37.860
People poke around in
lots of different places

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:40.020
and they hope,
again, they hope they

00:26:40.020 --> 00:26:44.140
find all the relevant
information, but they may not.

00:26:44.140 --> 00:26:48.000
The other thing is
things like, well, who

00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:50.220
has non-standard payment terms?

00:26:50.220 --> 00:26:52.260
How do you answer that?

00:26:52.260 --> 00:26:54.570
You get to read a
lot of documents.

00:26:54.570 --> 00:26:56.920
That's how it's done today.

00:26:56.920 --> 00:27:00.270
So then you look at
more complex questions

00:27:00.270 --> 00:27:03.780
like, which contracts have a
limit of liability for data

00:27:03.780 --> 00:27:05.820
breach above a $1 million?

00:27:05.820 --> 00:27:08.530
And now we're looking at
Document Understanding

00:27:08.530 --> 00:27:10.950
AI as a really,
really powerful tool

00:27:10.950 --> 00:27:15.000
to allow those natural
language questions to actually

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:16.110
be answered.

00:27:16.110 --> 00:27:19.770
So this particular use cases
where we worked very closely

00:27:19.770 --> 00:27:23.190
with Google on using the
knowledge capabilities

00:27:23.190 --> 00:27:26.190
that Sudherra introduced
earlier to really

00:27:26.190 --> 00:27:31.178
empower users to find this
type of information very,

00:27:31.178 --> 00:27:31.720
very quickly.

00:27:34.240 --> 00:27:36.510
So let me show you
a couple of examples

00:27:36.510 --> 00:27:39.750
that illustrate what
the UI looks like

00:27:39.750 --> 00:27:42.120
and what the experience
is for a user.

00:27:42.120 --> 00:27:45.180
So this is our simple,
I need all the contracts

00:27:45.180 --> 00:27:46.620
for a given customer.

00:27:46.620 --> 00:27:48.930
Now notice in this
case we're actually

00:27:48.930 --> 00:27:50.340
dealing with a salesperson.

00:27:50.340 --> 00:27:53.100
So another thing that
is really powerful here

00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:56.010
is that there are multiple
stakeholders potentially

00:27:56.010 --> 00:27:59.160
within your company that
need to access this content.

00:27:59.160 --> 00:28:01.170
And we can allow each
and every one of them

00:28:01.170 --> 00:28:05.790
to have the benefit of accessing
this rich information now

00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:07.090
very quickly.

00:28:07.090 --> 00:28:09.870
So in this particular
use case, you're

00:28:09.870 --> 00:28:13.950
going from poking around,
asking people, getting

00:28:13.950 --> 00:28:17.640
to different repositories,
to essentially one click.

00:28:17.640 --> 00:28:19.710
What we've done is
anything that we

00:28:19.710 --> 00:28:23.940
extract, we allow you to use
as a facet or as a widget.

00:28:23.940 --> 00:28:27.190
I can pin those on my UI.

00:28:27.190 --> 00:28:31.350
So if I'm usually looking
for things by customer name,

00:28:31.350 --> 00:28:33.570
I can actually just pin
that right to my UI.

00:28:33.570 --> 00:28:35.610
If I'm looking for
a certain customer,

00:28:35.610 --> 00:28:38.400
start typing in the name
or just click on the one

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:39.780
that I'm looking for.

00:28:39.780 --> 00:28:43.770
And all the documents that I
need are there with one click.

00:28:43.770 --> 00:28:47.070
So incredibly efficient
to get to what still

00:28:47.070 --> 00:28:49.185
is a fairly simple answer.

00:28:51.920 --> 00:28:55.340
Second one, a little
bit more complex.

00:28:55.340 --> 00:28:58.490
So now, who has
non-standard payment terms?

00:28:58.490 --> 00:29:02.570
This is actually quite a
common issue for companies.

00:29:02.570 --> 00:29:05.750
And it's incredibly
hard to get to.

